1
00:00:00,251 --> 00:00:12,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, another sutta here is Anguttara 1, book of 1, 268 to 276, and it's about a sotāpanna, a person with the right view, things that he's incapable of doing.

2
00:00:12,743 --> 00:00:19,690
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, because it is impossible, there is no opportunity that an individual accomplished in the view could approach

3
00:00:22,372 --> 00:00:25,854
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not possible.

4
00:00:26,254 --> 00:00:29,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's possible that Patujana could approach determination as permanent.

5
00:00:29,896 --> 00:00:30,697
[SPEAKER_00]: That is possible.

6
00:00:32,017 --> 00:00:32,738
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

7
00:00:32,838 --> 00:00:38,000
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no opportunity that any individual accomplished in view, Sotāpanna, could approach any determination as pleasurable.

8
00:00:38,681 --> 00:00:41,342
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible that he could approach anything itself.

9
00:00:43,843 --> 00:00:51,087
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible for him to take away the life of his mother, he could not take away the life of his father, and he could not take away the life of an arahant.

10
00:00:52,363 --> 00:00:55,187
[SPEAKER_00]: With a corrupt mind, he could not shed the blood of the Tathāgata.

11
00:00:55,427 --> 00:00:56,729
[SPEAKER_00]: He could not split the saṅgha.

12
00:00:56,949 --> 00:00:58,552
[SPEAKER_00]: He could not acknowledge another teacher.

13
00:00:58,612 --> 00:00:59,413
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not possible.

14
00:01:00,154 --> 00:01:02,998
[SPEAKER_00]: But because it is possible that an ordinary person could do these things.

15
00:01:05,296 --> 00:01:15,064
[SPEAKER_00]: So, obviously, this statement is made by the Buddha, who is much further seeing than even the most accomplished arahant.

16
00:01:15,924 --> 00:01:24,571
[SPEAKER_00]: But certain things here can be verified, obviously, for oneself, without needing supernatural powers and so on to see.

17
00:01:26,622 --> 00:01:39,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And those things are the impossibility of taking any saṅkhāra as permanent, impossibility of taking any saṅkhāra as pleasurable, and impossibility of approaching anything as self on account of it.

18
00:01:40,605 --> 00:01:42,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that impossible?

19
00:01:42,306 --> 00:01:44,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because it has been thoroughly understood.

20
00:01:45,646 --> 00:01:47,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoroughly understood in a sense that

21
00:01:50,264 --> 00:02:00,171
[SPEAKER_00]: These characteristics of impermanence and pleasurable... suffering, unpleasurability, are the inherent characteristics of saṅkhāras.

22
00:02:00,272 --> 00:02:04,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to understand impermanence and dukkha, you need to understand what saṅkhāras are.

23
00:02:06,248 --> 00:02:16,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could even see, simply based on, like, how these... how the word saṅkhara is translated in the modern, kind of, sutra translations, there's many, many views on the topics.

24
00:02:17,252 --> 00:02:21,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a subtle thing to understand, and much more easier to misunderstand.

25
00:02:23,326 --> 00:02:34,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't understand what saṅkharas are, you will not be able to then truly see impermanence and dukkha on the level where it needs to be seen for you to get the right view.

26
00:02:36,092 --> 00:02:49,938
[SPEAKER_00]: And another... another proof of this is how often this very similar passage that talks about saṅkharas, impermanence, is completely misquoted, misunderstood, misread even, while you're reading it.

27
00:02:50,842 --> 00:02:55,464
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha never said everything is impermanent, everything is suffering, everything is not-self.

28
00:02:56,085 --> 00:03:02,988
[SPEAKER_00]: He said every saṅkhara is impermanent, every saṅkhara is suffering, everything is not-self as a result of it.

29
00:03:03,329 --> 00:03:08,171
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sābe saṅkhara anicca, sābe saṅkhara dukkha, sābe dhamma anatta.

30
00:03:09,512 --> 00:03:13,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It just seems like, oh, well, there is some difference, but it's not a big deal.

31
00:03:13,394 --> 00:03:15,035
[SPEAKER_00]: It's absolutely fundamental deal.

32
00:03:16,202 --> 00:03:22,324
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like in the same sense with with patitsaṁpada, but we won't get into patitsaṁpada here now, because that's a talk in itself.

33
00:03:23,364 --> 00:03:32,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, you need to understand that there is a difference between your sense of self, which is always on the level of that dhamma, and things upon which sense of self depends.

34
00:03:33,247 --> 00:03:35,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what definition of saṅkhara is.

35
00:03:35,168 --> 00:03:37,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Inseparable from, determined by.

36
00:03:38,189 --> 00:03:40,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the translation here is determinations.

37
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:42,230
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example,

38
00:03:44,596 --> 00:03:57,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind state of, I don't know, anger or, let's take something positive, elated happiness on account of a prospect that you're facing, something pleasant coming your way.

39
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:02,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be able to have that mental state persisting?

40
00:04:02,801 --> 00:04:11,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you be able to be cherishing it, bound by it, enjoying it, looking forward to it, if your body were to die?

41
00:04:12,817 --> 00:04:32,129
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to die, if something were to happen to it, if you were to stop breathing, your heart were to stop, would it be possible for you to even conceive further experience of your sense domain and pleasure and joy, happiness and so on, without your life being a necessary basis from which any sense experience is inseparable from?

42
00:04:33,190 --> 00:04:35,051
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

43
00:04:35,071 --> 00:04:38,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, see, you recognize that and you can even make sense of it, even when you hear it just like this.

44
00:04:38,813 --> 00:04:39,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, of course.

45
00:04:39,453 --> 00:04:39,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, okay.

46
00:04:40,618 --> 00:04:59,373
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's not enough to recognize it once, or twice, because, as the Buddha said, and we mentioned before, ignorance is beginningless, which means you cultivating the wrong view, not seeing of saṅkhara, not regarding them, eh... impermanent and unpleasurable, but instead regarding them as permanent and pleasurable.

47
00:05:01,262 --> 00:05:06,965
[SPEAKER_00]: You will then simply not be able to approve that just by seeing it once or twice.

48
00:05:07,625 --> 00:05:10,846
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, as we said that before, dhamma cannot be done accidentally.

49
00:05:10,866 --> 00:05:14,908
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot stumble upon it and have this special experience that they make you an arahant and so on.

50
00:05:15,528 --> 00:05:17,369
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to be drilled repetitively.

51
00:05:17,469 --> 00:05:20,470
[SPEAKER_00]: That would have meant it's like gradual shelving of the seed.

52
00:05:20,811 --> 00:05:22,731
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes time and repetition.

53
00:05:22,751 --> 00:05:26,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like wearing off the handle of the axe and so on through usage.

54
00:05:27,696 --> 00:05:30,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you cannot do it accidentally, because you need to keep doing it.

55
00:05:31,017 --> 00:05:39,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order to keep doing something, you need to have had reason beforehand that it's worth doing and repeating and committing to it.

56
00:05:40,039 --> 00:05:41,060
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the place of faith.

57
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,160
[SPEAKER_00]: We covered that before.

58
00:05:44,081 --> 00:05:55,664
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order for you to then see the determinations as impermanent, you need to then accept that presently there is an experience of whatever that mood is, whatever feeling is,

59
00:05:56,687 --> 00:06:06,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever perception is, at the time, and then see it as undermined, see it as... see it undermined simultaneously by something else, that's there.

60
00:06:07,213 --> 00:06:25,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Such as, see, the fact that you're not in control over life in your body, so to speak, the life of your body, means that undermines any prospect of your sense engagement, your life, your career, your family, everything you go through life, is undermined by the fact that you could not be alive next moment.

61
00:06:26,630 --> 00:06:29,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's an unpleasant thing to do, to admit that to yourself.

62
00:06:30,153 --> 00:06:31,514
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the training comes in.

63
00:06:31,534 --> 00:06:38,299
[SPEAKER_00]: The virtue that will prepare you to, like, handle it, and then drilling and reflection off of that contemplation and recollecting it.

64
00:06:38,319 --> 00:06:39,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, death and so on.

65
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:49,788
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's basically you retraining yourself to not regard your most fundamental saṅkhara of your body as permanent, as pleasurable.

66
00:06:51,615 --> 00:06:56,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, if you ignore that, you then automatically regarding it as permanently pleasurable.

67
00:06:56,781 --> 00:06:57,042
[SPEAKER_00]: How?

68
00:06:57,122 --> 00:06:58,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the example I gave.

69
00:06:59,084 --> 00:07:03,169
[SPEAKER_00]: The joyful prospect you're looking forward to, you don't question it.

70
00:07:03,270 --> 00:07:04,351
[SPEAKER_00]: You take it for granted.

71
00:07:05,537 --> 00:07:10,781
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you take that as yourself, as your defining experience at the time that you're going for.

72
00:07:11,562 --> 00:07:15,325
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't question it, you don't even try to reflect on it.

73
00:07:15,926 --> 00:07:18,868
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what then happens to that body, which is there, by the way?

74
00:07:19,008 --> 00:07:21,610
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot abandon saṅkharas or replace them.

75
00:07:22,971 --> 00:07:30,217
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your body, as a fundamental necessary basis for any experience of the senses, elating experience or depressing, doesn't matter, has to be there.

76
00:07:30,738 --> 00:07:31,959
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're not questioning.

77
00:07:32,884 --> 00:07:34,185
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not having a second thought.

78
00:07:34,205 --> 00:07:37,626
[SPEAKER_00]: You're taking for granted the related experience that you're presented with.

79
00:07:37,847 --> 00:07:41,128
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it, you crave it, you delight in it, you act upon it.

80
00:07:41,408 --> 00:07:43,029
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what happens to saṅkhara then?

81
00:07:45,070 --> 00:07:46,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they're put second.

82
00:07:47,131 --> 00:07:51,293
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not seen as that because of which you are able to have the related experience.

83
00:07:51,894 --> 00:07:58,197
[SPEAKER_00]: They're seen that which is secondary, which is sort of subordinate to your related experience.

84
00:08:00,539 --> 00:08:05,526
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you put yourself first, and then these saṅkharas then become tools of yourself.

85
00:08:06,127 --> 00:08:13,897
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of saṅkharas undermining your sense of self, that will then liberate you, saṅkharas become used by the sense of self.

86
00:08:14,771 --> 00:08:18,294
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when saṅkharas are regarded as permanent and pleasurable.

87
00:08:18,374 --> 00:08:32,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of impermanence and discomfort of saṅkharas undermining the pleasurability of sense of self, they get to be seen through the pleasurability, gratuitous, gratuitously assumed pleasurability of the sense of self.

88
00:08:33,285 --> 00:08:36,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, eh... that's why is that opposing.

89
00:08:36,047 --> 00:08:39,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you need to see that which is permanent as impermanent.

90
00:08:40,069 --> 00:08:43,572
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to see that which you regard as pleasurable as not pleasurable.

91
00:08:44,661 --> 00:08:56,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to learn how to see saṅkharas for what they are, as... or rather be able to admit to yourself that it's fundamentally unpleasurable, the fact that you're not in control of your body, even if you can use it now for gaining all the pleasure you want.

92
00:08:57,546 --> 00:09:06,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing the nature of that discomfort, that impermanence, I cannot own that, will then undermine your joy on that level of sense of self.

93
00:09:06,693 --> 00:09:16,560
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's unpleasant, so people turn away from this pleasure by default, which then makes you regard all these obvious things that are not in your control, that are uncomfortable.

94
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:21,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Even that elated pleasure that has arisen, you know that it cannot last for, in a, like,

95
00:09:22,544 --> 00:09:25,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Forever, in a sense of that you will never experience pain again.

96
00:09:25,168 --> 00:09:32,599
[SPEAKER_00]: You know you will, but you take it for granted at the face value that everything else is seen through that pleasure of of that you took for granted.

97
00:09:33,428 --> 00:09:34,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Not other way around.

98
00:09:34,369 --> 00:09:36,350
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's learning to turn that picture upside down.

99
00:09:37,111 --> 00:09:45,658
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why in all those suttas, when the Buddha would teach the dhamma, and people would understand, they would say to him, oh, wow, it's like turning that which has been placed upside down, turning it back rightly again.

100
00:09:46,378 --> 00:09:49,901
[SPEAKER_00]: That which I used to put second is first, now it needs to be put first is second.

101
00:09:49,921 --> 00:09:51,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So, saṅkharas are first.

102
00:09:51,923 --> 00:09:58,808
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to put them as first, as a necessary basis, because of which you wouldn't be able to have experience of joy, nor pain, nor anything else in between.

103
00:10:00,770 --> 00:10:08,092
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you start looking then at the characteristics of this, like, what's the characteristic of your presently living, enduring body, right here, right now?

104
00:10:09,473 --> 00:10:16,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you haven't had any say in its arising, you have no say in its organs operating.

105
00:10:16,855 --> 00:10:22,616
[SPEAKER_00]: You can use it, you can move it, you can control it, but fundamentally, in itself, it's not in your control.

106
00:10:23,857 --> 00:10:26,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing that means seeing determinations as impermanent.

107
00:10:26,457 --> 00:10:27,718
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what the right view is.

108
00:10:29,002 --> 00:10:34,044
[SPEAKER_00]: When you recognize that, to the extent... sufficient extent, you have developed the right view.

109
00:10:34,524 --> 00:10:36,144
[SPEAKER_00]: You... that's what sotāpati is.

110
00:10:37,044 --> 00:10:41,366
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that person will be simply unable to forget that.

111
00:10:41,606 --> 00:10:44,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is your very core of your being, of your thought, of your mind.

112
00:10:44,967 --> 00:10:48,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not something that you occasionally think and then you sort of forget about it.

113
00:10:51,547 --> 00:11:02,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why a sotāpanna will be unable to approach for granted determination as pleasurable impermanence, because determination for him now signifies impermanence.

114
00:11:03,318 --> 00:11:10,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Determination now signifies discomfort, dukkha, which that means any experience cannot be taken itself.

115
00:11:12,053 --> 00:11:16,635
[SPEAKER_00]: And as for taking away the life of his mother, father, arahant, blood of Tathāgata, means intentionally.

116
00:11:16,675 --> 00:11:21,698
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot intentionally override the significance of recognition of his mother.

117
00:11:22,778 --> 00:11:25,360
[SPEAKER_00]: He cannot, with a corrupt mind, shed the blood of Tathāgata.

118
00:11:25,580 --> 00:11:26,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

119
00:11:26,260 --> 00:11:32,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the significance of that act is just inconceivable for him to willingly, consciously make a choice and do that.

120
00:11:32,803 --> 00:11:33,604
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just impossible.

121
00:11:34,084 --> 00:11:35,425
[SPEAKER_00]: He could not split the saṅgha.

122
00:11:35,585 --> 00:11:36,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

123
00:11:37,346 --> 00:11:39,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, willingly try to make schism.

124
00:11:39,587 --> 00:11:40,607
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because he's part of it.

125
00:11:40,627 --> 00:11:41,348
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just impossible.

126
00:11:42,492 --> 00:11:44,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Or that he could acknowledge another teacher.

127
00:11:44,857 --> 00:11:46,039
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not possible either.

128
00:11:47,602 --> 00:11:48,424
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's literally so.

129
00:11:48,785 --> 00:11:53,554
[SPEAKER_00]: You have arrived at uprooting the entire existence through following the Buddha's instruction.

130
00:11:54,612 --> 00:12:02,998
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now that you can, like, follow another teacher, means now that you can dismiss that, and follow some completely diametrically opposed teaching, it's impossible.

131
00:12:03,558 --> 00:12:14,345
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inconceivable, because within that diametrically opposed teaching, you will still see, saṅkharas are impermanent, saṅkharas are uncomfortable, everything else, as a result, that depends upon them, cannot be taken itself.

132
00:12:15,186 --> 00:12:21,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you're still, whatever teaching either you see, you're just gonna see it through the framework of dhamma that you have understood.

133
00:12:22,206 --> 00:12:25,389
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you become your own guide, as the other suttas say.

134
00:12:25,989 --> 00:12:27,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody can be my guide.

135
00:12:27,090 --> 00:12:27,851
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm my own guide.

136
00:12:28,131 --> 00:12:32,635
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the reference point, the dhamma I, as the Buddha referred to, that now I can see.

137
00:12:33,095 --> 00:12:34,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is according to dhamma.

138
00:12:34,377 --> 00:12:35,758
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not according to the dhamma.

139
00:12:36,570 --> 00:12:53,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as the Buddha said in another sutta, even if the whole world comes to that person and says, no, the Buddha is wrong, you should not follow the dhamma, he, like, you know, he might not say anything, you know, if they will harass him and so on, but internally it's impossible for him to basically agree with them.

140
00:12:53,881 --> 00:12:59,405
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just impossible for him, even if the whole world tries to persuade him, he will know that the Buddha is right and the world is wrong.

141
00:13:00,065 --> 00:13:01,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he verified it for himself.

142
00:13:02,406 --> 00:13:03,127
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

